year every  year with mfa's it's a it's a really  hard career  you've got to really do it because  because you love it um so  the vast majority of artists that we  look at we don't consider  investment grade um and and that's  that's  you know even artists that are that are  culturally significant so for example  one of the things that we learned very  early on is that appreciation rates by  individual artists tend to follow  recency meaning if you invest in a  rembrandt today  rembrandt is just not that fashionable  so you won't lose money investing that  painting  but your investment will probably only  grow at one or two percent a year  maybe in context with inflation so so  in today's world we really focus on art  created after world war ii  and we tend to focus on artists that  have momentum we tend to focus on  artists that have  markets above 50 million dollars a year  um it's it's you know it's  very hard to to view sort of ten  thousand dollar paintings twenty  thousand dollar paintings  even in today's world hundred thousand  dollar paintings as as  investment grade because it's just too  unpredictable is there something about  the masterworks approach  that is almost like democratizing  access to the space in the sense that  with with fractional investment  you're you're opening this up to large  swaths of people that could never have  access otherwise  yeah i mean i i use that exact exact  word all the time  and i think what's really cool here is  right we we can take a painting that's a  culturally significant painting  we can have a couple thousand people  essentially purchase the painting  and then we can take the painting to an  institution or a museum hanging out on  the wall  and it doesn't go out of circulation so  it's a it's a really nice way for people  to gain exposure to the asset class  and also for artists to continue  exhibiting their work publicly  super interesting we've been speaking  with scott lynn who's the founder and  ceo of masterworks about which you can  learn more at  masterworks.io scott i really appreciate  your time  thanks david
 

